  "data": {
    "accession": "PXD006359",
    "title": "Identification of Siglec ligands in human B cell",
    "additionalAttributes": [],
    "projectDescription": "Siglecs are a family of receptor-type glycan recognition proteins (lectins) involved in self - nonself discrimination by the immune system. Identification of Siglec ligand(s) is necessary to understand how Siglec-ligand interaction translates to biological outcomes, but is challenging because the interaction is weak. To facilitate identification of Siglec ligands, we adopted proximity labeling method based on tyramide radicalization principle.",
    "sampleProcessingProtocol": "Cells (BJAB B cell) that express Siglec ligand were labeled with Siglec-peroxidase complex and incubated with biotin tyramide and hydrogen peroxide, to generate short-lived tyramide radical that covalently label the proteins near the Siglec-peroxidase complex. After purifying the biotinylated complex and eluting by photocleavage, the eluted proteins were separated by short SDS-PAGE and performed by in-gel tryptic digestion, C18 ziptip desalting, and LC-MS/MS analysis.",
    "dataProcessingProtocol": "All data files were processed by Proteome Discoverer 2.1 and peptide identification was performed by Mascot search engine (version 2.3.2) and SEQUEST search engine against the Swiss-Prot database from Human with a percolator (strict false discovery rate (FDR) of 0.01 and a relaxed FDR of 0.05). For label-free quantitation, the peak area (i.e. area under the curve) of each precursor ion was calculated from extracted ion chromatogram during data processing by using the Precursor Ions Area Detector node with mass precision 2 ppm. The abundance of identified protein was calculated from top 3 of all unique and razor peptides in Peptide and Protein Quantifier node. The relative quantitation was further compared with the protein abundance from control and experimental samples.",
